-- Version 10 → 11: every project gains a "Duplicate" resolution.
--
-- 4.sqm's twin, and its comment is the long version of this one: no schema
-- changes at all, because `resolutions` already stores the vocabulary as rows,
-- one per project, so a new default is data. MigrationTest is blind to it by
-- construction — it compares PRAGMA output, which is identical either side of an
-- INSERT — so `11.db` is a byte-for-byte-equivalent shape to `10.db` and exists
-- only because the version moved. The row is covered by ResolutionMigrationTest
-- instead.
--
-- The create path does not run this. A fresh volume gets its resolutions from
-- ProjectRepository's DEFAULT_RESOLUTIONS at the moment each project is created,
-- and that list now carries the same name. This file is the other half: the
-- projects on the deployed volume that already exist and would otherwise keep
-- their two resolutions forever.
--
-- ── Why the end, and not beside "Done" ─────────────────────────────────────
--
-- `position` is not decoration here: the board groups closed issues by it, and
-- Resolutions.sq's convention is 0-first ascending. Inserting in the middle would
-- shuffle every existing resolution one to the right on every board — a silent
-- re-grouping of issues nobody asked to have re-grouped, on a volume serving real
-- boards. Appending changes the position of nothing that already exists, which is
-- why this file has no UPDATE at all and 4.sqm needed one.
--
-- MAX(position) + 1 is read per project rather than assumed to be 2: a project
-- may have added resolutions of its own through the settings dialog, and a
-- constant would collide with one. The COALESCE tail is for a project with no
-- resolutions at all — unreachable, because seeding is one transaction with the
-- project insert, but `position` is NOT NULL and an unreachable NULL here would
-- be a failed migration on startup rather than one bad row.
--
-- ── Why the guard ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
--
-- `UNIQUE (project_id, name)` on a COLLATE NOCASE column. A project whose admin
-- already made a "Duplicate" resolution by hand — or a "duplicate" one — would
-- violate it, and a violation here is not a bad row: it aborts the migration,
-- which aborts startup, on the deployed volume, for everyone.

INSERT INTO resolutions (project_id, name, position)
SELECT
    p.id,
    'Duplicate',
    COALESCE((SELECT MAX(r.position) + 1 FROM resolutions r WHERE r.project_id = p.id), 0)
FROM projects p
WHERE NOT EXISTS (
    SELECT 1 FROM resolutions e
    WHERE e.project_id = p.id AND e.name = 'Duplicate'
);
